// @status: GENERAL
// @invariant: non_exhaustive_match
//
// Structurally different from R-007: the defect is in a match that appears as
// another match's ARM RESULT, and the outer match is exhaustive. The
// diagnostic points at the inner one.
//
// This file spent a few hours misfiled as `slips-through.pw`, recorded as a
// gap in the exhaustiveness checker. It was not. The first version used
// constructor names `OrderState` does not have — `Placed`, `Preparing`,
// `Ready` — so the outer match's patterns lowered to nothing meaningful and
// the inner match was never reached. The witness tested the checker's
// behaviour on a program that did not typecheck, and reported the result as a
// property of the checker.
//
// What found it: `PW0603`, the constructor-arity rule added in the same hour,
// which is the first repair layer for the panic this whole file descends from.
// A witness has to be a valid program in every respect except the one it is
// about, and nothing had been checking that.

module generality.match_nested

import domain.{ OrderState }

fn describe(a: OrderState, b: OrderState) -> String !{} {
    match a {
        Draft => match b {
            Draft => "both draft"
            Pricing => "one pricing"
        }
        Pricing => "pricing"
        AuthorizingPayment => "authorizing"
        Submitting => "submitting"
        SearchingForDasher => "searching"
        Confirmed(c) => "confirmed"
        InProgress(p) => "in progress"
        Delivered(r) => "delivered"
        Cancelled(reason) => "cancelled"
        Failed(f) => "failed"
    }
}
